# Flutter/Dart AI Agent Coding Rules

## Core Principles

### 1. Code Organization & Architecture
- Follow **MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)** pattern: separate presentation and data layers only
- Use **feature-first** folder structure instead of layer-first
- Keep each file under 300 lines; split into multiple files if longer
- One widget per file for complex widgets (except small helper widgets)
- Group related files in features: `lib/features/feature_name/{data, presentation}`
- **NO domain layer** - business logic goes in ViewModels (ChangeNotifier classes)
- Use **GetIt** for dependency injection - never manually create instances
- Use **GoRouter** for all navigation through `app_router.dart`

### 2. State Management (MVVM Pattern with Provider)
- Use **Provider with ChangeNotifier** as the ViewModel layer in MVVM
- **Always use ChangeNotifier** for state management
- **ViewModel contains all business logic** - no separate domain/use case layer needed
- Never mix state management solutions in the same feature
- ViewModels directly call repositories and handle business logic
- **Inject ViewModels using GetIt** in the router, never create them manually
- **Provide ViewModels at the route level** in `app_router.dart` using `ChangeNotifierProvider`
- Always dispose ViewModels properly (auto-dispose when using ChangeNotifierProvider)
- One ViewModel per feature/screen (avoid shared ViewModels unless truly needed)
- **Constructor injection**: All ViewModel dependencies must be injected via constructor using GetIt
- Use `notifyListeners()` to update UI after state changes

**MVVM Layers:**
- **Model**: Data models and repositories in `data/` folder
- **View**: UI widgets and screens in `presentation/screens/` and `presentation/widgets/`
- **ViewModel**: ChangeNotifier classes in `presentation/view_models/` folder (contains business logic)

**State Management Pattern:**
```dart
class UserViewModel extends ChangeNotifier {
  UserState _state = const UserState.initial();
  UserState get state => _state;

  void _setState(UserState newState) {
    _state = newState;
    notifyListeners();
  }
}
```

### 3. Widget Best Practices
- Prefer **composition over inheritance**
- Extract reusable widgets into separate files in `lib/widgets/` or `lib/core/widgets/`
- Use `const` constructors wherever possible for better performance
- Keep `build()` methods simple and readable (max 50 lines)
- Extract complex widget trees into separate methods or widgets
- Use `StatelessWidget` by default; only use `StatefulWidget` when necessary

### 4. Naming Conventions
- **Files**: snake_case (e.g., `user_profile_screen.dart`, `user_view_model.dart`)
- **Classes**: PascalCase (e.g., `UserProfileScreen`, `UserViewModel`)
- **Variables/Functions**: camelCase (e.g., `userName`, `getUserData()`)
- **Constants**: lowerCamelCase with `k` prefix (e.g., `kPrimaryColor`)
- **Private members**: prefix with underscore (e.g., `_privateMethod()`)
- Suffix widgets with their type: `LoginScreen`, `UserCard`, `LoadingButton`
- Suffix ViewModels with `ViewModel`: `UserViewModel`, `ProfileViewModel`

### 5. Code Structure Within Files
```dart
// 1. Imports (dart: first, then package:, then relative)
import 'dart:async';

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:provider/provider.dart';

import '../models/user.dart';
import '../services/auth_service.dart';

// 2. Class declaration with documentation
/// Detailed description of what this class does
class MyWidget extends StatelessWidget {
  // 3. Constants
  static const double kDefaultPadding = 16.0;
  
  // 4. Final fields (constructor parameters)
  final String title;
  final VoidCallback? onPressed;
  
  // 5. Constructor
  const MyWidget({
    super.key,
    required this.title,
    this.onPressed,
  });
  
  // 6. Overridden methods
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Container();
  }
  
  // 7. Public methods
  void publicMethod() {}
  
  // 8. Private methods
  void _privateMethod() {}
}
```

### 6. Error Handling & Validation
- Always handle errors with try-catch blocks for async operations
- **Always use `Either` type from dartz package** for operations that can fail (Left for failure, Right for success)
- Return `Either<Failure, SuccessType>` from all repository methods
- Create custom Failure classes for different error types (ServerFailure, CacheFailure, etc.)
- Validate user input immediately and show clear error messages
- Never use empty catch blocks; always log errors
- Use `assert()` for development-time checks

### 7. Async Programming
- Always use `async`/`await` instead of `.then()` for readability
- Add `unawaited()` from `dart:async` for fire-and-forget operations
- Use `Future.wait()` for parallel async operations
- Implement proper loading states for all async operations
- Cancel streams and futures in `dispose()`

### 8. Performance Optimization
- Use `const` constructors wherever possible
- Implement `ListView.builder` for long lists, never `ListView()` with many children
- Use `RepaintBoundary` for expensive widgets that don't change often
- Use `Consumer` or `Selector` to rebuild only necessary parts of the widget tree
- Cache expensive computations in ViewModel as private fields
- Use `Image.network` with `cacheHeight` and `cacheWidth` parameters

### 9. Code Documentation
- Add dartdoc comments (`///`) for all public APIs
- Document complex logic with inline comments (`//`)
- Include example usage in documentation for complex widgets/functions
- Document why something is done, not just what (code shows what)

### 10. Testing Requirements
- Write unit tests for all business logic in ViewModels
- Write widget tests for custom widgets
- Name test files with `_test.dart` suffix
- Use descriptive test names: `test('should return user when login is successful')`
- Aim for >80% code coverage on business logic

### 11. Dependency Management
- Keep dependencies up to date
- Use exact versions for critical packages
- Prefer well-maintained, popular packages
- Add dependency reasons as comments in `pubspec.yaml`
- Group dependencies logically in `pubspec.yaml`

